# The Three Language Planes

Track Binocle is a Docker-first monorepo that deliberately spans **three
languages**. Each language owns a *plane* — a horizontal slice of the system with
one clear responsibility. The rule of thumb: pick the language that best fits the
concern, not the concern that best fits one language.

> [!info] One concern, one plane, one language
> A plane is not a service — it is a *responsibility boundary*. Many services can
> live in the same plane, but a service never straddles two.

## The split at a glance

| Plane | Language | Owns | Lives in |
|:------|:--------:|:-----|:---------|
| **Application** | TypeScript | Everything a user touches | root compose frontends |
| **Control** | Go | *Who* can do *what* | `grobase/src/control-plane/` |
| **Data** | Rust | Moving bytes, fast | `grobase/src/data-plane-router/` + realtime |

Each plane has its own page:

- [The Application Plane](application-plane.xmd) — React, Astro, the bridges.
- [The Control Plane](control-plane.xmd) — provisioning, auth, tenancy, metering.
- [The Data Plane](data-plane.xmd) — query execution, DB drivers, realtime routing.

## Why three languages, not one

> [!example] Right tool, right layer
> **TypeScript** shares types across editor and website and moves fast on UI.
> **Go** is boring, safe, and great at concurrent request handling for the plane
> that gates access. **Rust** gives predictable, low-latency data movement with no
> garbage-collection pauses on the hot path.

- **TypeScript (application)** — the React block editor *osionos*, the Astro
  marketing-and-auth site *opposite-osiris*, the bridging services, and the Mail
  & Calendar apps.
- **Go (control)** — provisioning, the auth gateway, tenancy & secrets, metering
  and billing.
- **Rust (data)** — high-performance query execution, database drivers,
  transaction handling, and realtime event routing.

## How the planes are wired

The application plane runs as the **frontends-only root compose stack**. The
control and data planes live together inside **grobase**, a standalone
backend-as-a-service the frontends reach over an external Docker network.

```text
  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Application plane  (TypeScript)           │
  │  osionos · opposite-osiris · bridges       │  root docker-compose.yml
  └───────────────────┬───────────────────────┘
                      │  container-name DNS
  ┌───────────────────▼───────────────────────┐
  │  grobase BaaS  (Kong front door)           │
  │  ├─ Control plane  (Go)                    │
  │  └─ Data plane     (Rust)                   │  separate docker project
  └───────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

## The discipline that ties them together

Every major change across the planes follows one rule: **shadow → parity →
cutover**. New code runs in parallel with the old, validation proves they behave
identically, and only then does the new path take over. Deletion is always the
last step.

> [!tip] Read next
> The [shadow → parity → cutover](shadow-parity-cutover.xmd) discipline is what
> makes cross-plane changes safe. Start there once you understand the planes.
